[{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q1","number":1,"question":"<p>Using Simpson's rule, calculate an approximation to the integral</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_0^2 e^{-x^3}\\,dx\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>for each of the following, giving your answer to 6 decimal places:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Three ordinates.</p></li><li><p>Five ordinates.</p></li><li><p>Seven ordinates.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\dfrac{2-0}{2}=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The ordinates are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1,e^{-1},e^{-8}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=0,1,2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Thus</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_0^2e^{-x^3}\\,dx\\approx\\frac13(1)\\{1+e^{-8}+4e^{-1}\\}=0.823951\\quad(6\\text{ DP}).\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\dfrac{2-0}{4}=0.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The ordinates at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=0,0.5,1,1.5,2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1,e^{-0.125},e^{-1},e^{-3.375},e^{-8}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Hence</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_0^2e^{-x^3}\\,dx\\approx\\frac13(0.5)\\{1+e^{-8}+4(e^{-0.125}+e^{-3.375})+2e^{-1}\\}=0.900492\\quad(6\\text{ DP}).\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\dfrac{2-0}{6}=\\dfrac13\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The ordinates at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=0,\\frac13,\\frac23,1,\\frac43,\\frac53,2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"1,e^{-1/27},e^{-8/27},e^{-1},e^{-64/27},e^{-125/27},e^{-8}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>Therefore</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}\\int_0^2e^{-x^3}\\,dx&amp;\\approx\\frac13\\cdot\\frac13\\{1+e^{-8}+4(e^{-1/27}+e^{-1}+e^{-125/27})+2(e^{-8/27}+e^{-64/27})\\}\\\\&amp;=0.893275\\quad(6\\text{ DP}).\\end{aligned}\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q2","number":2,"question":"<p>Using Simpson's rule, calculate an approximation to the integral</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_1^2 \\frac{1}{1+\\ln x}\\,dx\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>for each of the following, giving your answer to 6 decimal places:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Two strips.</p></li><li><p>Four strips.</p></li><li><p>Six strips.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\dfrac{2-1}{2}=0.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and the ordinates are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1,\\dfrac1{1+\\ln1.5},\\dfrac1{1+\\ln2}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Thus</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_1^2\\frac{1}{1+\\ln x}\\,dx\\approx\\frac13(0.5)\\left\\{1+\\frac1{1+\\ln2}+4\\left(\\frac1{1+\\ln1.5}\\right)\\right\\}=0.739442\\quad(6\\text{ DP}).\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\dfrac{2-1}{4}=0.25\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Hence</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}\\int_1^2\\frac{1}{1+\\ln x}\\,dx\\approx\\frac13(0.25)\\{&amp;1+\\frac1{1+\\ln2}+4(\\frac1{1+\\ln1.25}+\\frac1{1+\\ln1.75})\\\\&amp;+2(\\frac1{1+\\ln1.5})\\}=0.737386\\quad(6\\text{ DP}).\\end{aligned}\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\dfrac{2-1}{6}=\\dfrac16\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Therefore</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}\\int_1^2\\frac{1}{1+\\ln x}\\,dx\\approx\\frac13\\cdot\\frac16\\{&amp;1+\\frac1{1+\\ln2}+4(\\frac1{1+\\ln(7/6)}+\\frac1{1+\\ln1.5}+\\frac1{1+\\ln(11/6)})\\\\&amp;+2(\\frac1{1+\\ln(4/3)}+\\frac1{1+\\ln(5/3)})\\}=0.737212\\quad(6\\text{ DP}).\\end{aligned}\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q3","number":3,"question":"<p>The table below shows the velocity <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"v\" data-display=\"false\"></span> m s<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"^{-1}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> of a car every 2 seconds.</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\begin{array}{c|ccccc}\nt&amp;0&amp;2&amp;4&amp;6&amp;8\\\\\\hline\nv&amp;0&amp;5.6&amp;9.8&amp;13.0&amp;15.2\n\\end{array}\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>Use Simpson's rule to estimate the distance travelled in the first 8 seconds.</p><p><strong>Prerequisite knowledge for Q4: Hyperbolic Functions</strong></p>","steps":["<p>The width is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> seconds. Using the velocities in the table,</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\text{distance}\\approx\\frac13(2)\\{0+15.2+4(5.6+13.0)+2(9.8)\\}=\\frac13(2)(109.2)=72.8\\text{ m}.\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q4","number":4,"question":"<p>Use Simpson's rule with five ordinates to find an estimate for</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_0^1 \\cosh x\\,dx,\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>giving your answer to three significant figures.</p>","steps":["<p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\dfrac{1-0}{4}=0.25\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The ordinates are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1,\\cosh0.25,\\cosh0.5,\\cosh0.75,\\cosh1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_0^1\\cosh x\\,dx\\approx\\frac13(0.25)\\{1+\\cosh1+4(\\cosh0.25+\\cosh0.75)+2\\cosh0.5\\}=1.18\\quad(3\\text{ SF}).\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q5","number":5,"question":"<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Use Simpson's rule with four strips of equal width to find an estimate for</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_{0.1}^{0.5}\\frac{\\tan x}{x}\\,dx,\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>giving your answer to 3 significant figures.</p></li><li><p>Explain how you could find an improved estimate.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\dfrac{0.5-0.1}{4}=0.1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Thus</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0.1}^{0.5}\\frac{\\tan x}{x}\\,dx\\approx\\frac13(0.1)\\{&amp;\\frac{\\tan0.1}{0.1}+\\frac{\\tan0.5}{0.5}\\\\&amp;+4(\\frac{\\tan0.2}{0.2}+\\frac{\\tan0.4}{0.4})+2(\\frac{\\tan0.3}{0.3})\\}=0.415\\quad(3\\text{ SF}).\\end{aligned}\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p>Increase the number of strips used.</p></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q6","number":6,"question":"<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Use Simpson's rule with three ordinates to estimate</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_0^\\pi e^{\\cos x}\\,dx,\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>giving your answer to four decimal places.</p></li><li><p>Explain how Simpson's rule could be used to find a more accurate estimate of the integral in part (a).</p></li></ol>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\dfrac{\\pi-0}{2}=\\dfrac\\pi2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The ordinates are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e,e^0=1,e^{-1}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_1^2\\frac{1}{1+\\ln x}\\,dx\\approx\\frac13\\cdot\\frac\\pi2\\{e+e^{-1}+4\\}=3.7103\\quad(4\\text{ DP}).\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p>Increase the number of ordinates used.</p></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q7","number":7,"question":"<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Use Simpson's rule with four strips to find an estimate for</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_0^1 e^x\\,dx,\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>giving your answer to five significant figures.</p></li><li><p>Find the exact value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 e^x\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Find the relative error of your approximation from part (a).</p></li></ol><p><strong>Prerequisite knowledge for Q8: Trapezium Rule</strong></p>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\dfrac{1-0}{4}=0.25\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_0^1e^x\\,dx\\approx\\frac13(0.25)\\{1+e+4(e^{0.25}+e^{0.75})+2e^{0.5}\\}=1.7183\\quad(5\\text{ SF}).\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^1e^x\\,dx=[e^x]_0^1=e-1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\frac{1.7183-(e-1)}{e-1}=0.0000106\" data-display=\"false\"></span> (3 SF).</p></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q8","number":8,"question":"<p>It is given that</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"I=\\int_0^1 (x^2+e^{2x})\\,dx.\" data-display=\"true\"></span><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find an estimate for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"I\" data-display=\"false\"></span> using the trapezium rule with four strips, giving your answer to four decimal places.</p></li><li><p>Find an estimate for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"I\" data-display=\"false\"></span> using Simpson's rule with four strips, giving your answer to four decimal places.</p></li><li><p>Find the exact value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"I\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol><p><strong>Prerequisite knowledge for Q9: Reversing the Chain Rule or Integration by Substitution</strong></p>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>For the trapezium rule with <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=0.25\" data-display=\"false\"></span>,</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"I\\approx\\frac12(0.25)\\{1+1+e^2+2(0.0625+e^{0.5}+0.25+e+0.5625+e^{1.5})\\}=3.6046\\quad(4\\text{ DP}).\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p>For Simpson's rule,</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"I\\approx\\frac13(0.25)\\{1+1+e^2+4(0.0625+e^{0.5}+0.5625+e^{1.5})+2(0.25+e)\\}=3.5289\\quad(4\\text{ DP}).\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle I=\\left[\\frac{x^3}{3}+\\frac12e^{2x}\\right]_0^1=\\left(\\frac13+\\frac12e^2\\right)-\\frac12=\\frac12e^2-\\frac16\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q9","number":9,"question":"<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Use Simpson's rule with two strips to find an estimate for</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_0^2 x\\sqrt{2x^2+1}\\,dx,\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>giving your answer to four decimal places.</p></li><li><p>Find the exact value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^2 x\\sqrt{2x^2+1}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Find the relative error of your approximation from part (a).</p></li></ol>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and the ordinates for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=0,1,2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0,\\sqrt3,6\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Hence</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_0^2x\\sqrt{2x^2+1}\\,dx\\approx\\frac13(1)\\{0+6+4\\sqrt3\\}=4.3094\\quad(4\\text{ DP}).\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^2x\\sqrt{2x^2+1}\\,dx=\\int_0^2x(2x^2+1)^{1/2}\\,dx=\\left[\\frac16(2x^2+1)^{3/2}\\right]_0^2=\\frac{27-1}{6}=\\frac{13}{3}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\frac{4.3094-13/3}{13/3}=-0.00552\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q10","number":10,"question":"<p>A curve has equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=\\cos(x^2)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Use Simpson's rule with four strips to find an estimate for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\cos(x^2)\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, giving your answer to four decimal places.</p></li><li><p>The graph with equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=\\cos(x^2)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is stretched with scale factor 5 parallel to the <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\" data-display=\"false\"></span>-axis and then translated by 1 parallel to the <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span>-axis to give the graph with equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=f(x)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Write down an expression for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(x)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Giving your answers to three decimal places, using your answer to part (a), state an estimate for:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\" type=\"i\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 f(x)\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol></li><li><p>Use Simpson's rule with four strips to find an estimate for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 f(x)\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=0.25\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and the ordinates are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1,\\cos0.0625,\\cos0.25,\\cos0.5625,\\cos1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Thus</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_0^1\\cos(x^2)\\,dx\\approx\\frac13(0.25)\\{1+\\cos1+4(\\cos0.0625+\\cos0.5625)+2\\cos0.25\\}=0.9045\\quad(4\\text{ DP}).\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(x)=5\\cos((x-1)^2)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>By the translation and vertical stretch, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 f(x)\\,dx=5\\int_0^1\\cos(x^2)\\,dx\\approx5(0.9045)=4.523\" data-display=\"false\"></span> (3 DP).</p></li><li><p>On <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"[1,2]\" data-display=\"false\"></span> the ordinates are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"5,5\\cos0.0625,5\\cos0.25,5\\cos0.5625,5\\cos1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Therefore</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}\\int_1^2f(x)\\,dx&amp;\\approx\\frac13(0.25)\\{5+5\\cos1+4(5\\cos0.0625+5\\cos0.5625)+2(5\\cos0.25)\\}\\\\&amp;=4.523\\quad(3\\text{ DP}).\\end{aligned}\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q11","number":11,"question":"<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Use Simpson's rule with five ordinates to find an approximation to</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_{0.1}^{0.3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^3}}\\,dx,\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>giving your answer to three decimal places.</p></li><li><p>Hence find an approximation to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_{0.1}^{0.3}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{1-x^3}}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\dfrac{0.3-0.1}{4}=0.05\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Then</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0.1}^{0.3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^3}}\\,dx&amp;\\approx\\frac13(0.05)\\{\\frac1{\\sqrt{1-0.1^3}}+\\frac1{\\sqrt{1-0.3^3}}\\\\&amp;+4(\\frac1{\\sqrt{1-0.15^3}}+\\frac1{\\sqrt{1-0.25^3}})+2(\\frac1{\\sqrt{1-0.2^3}})\\}\\\\&amp;=0.201\\quad(3\\text{ DP}).\\end{aligned}\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p>Multiplying by 5 gives</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_{0.1}^{0.3}\\frac5{\\sqrt{1-x^3}}\\,dx\\approx1.005\\quad(3\\text{ DP}).\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q12","number":12,"question":"<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Use Simpson's rule with five ordinates to find an approximation to</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_1^3\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\,dx,\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>giving your answer to three decimal places.</p></li><li><p>Hence find an approximation to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_1^3\\left(3-\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\right)\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol><p><strong>Prerequisite knowledge for Q13: Volume of Revolution</strong></p>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\dfrac{3-1}{4}=0.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Therefore</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}\\int_1^3\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\,dx\\approx\\frac13(0.5)\\{&amp;\\frac{\\sin1}{1}+\\frac{\\sin3}{3}\\\\&amp;+4(\\frac{\\sin1.5}{1.5}+\\frac{\\sin2.5}{2.5})+2(\\frac{\\sin2}{2})\\}=0.903\\quad(3\\text{ DP}).\\end{aligned}\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_1^3(3-\\frac{\\sin x}{x})\\,dx\\approx 3(3-1)-0.903=5.097\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q13","number":13,"question":"<p>A curve has equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=x\\ln(x+2)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The region bounded by the curve, the lines <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, and the <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span>-axis is rotated <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"360^\\circ\" data-display=\"false\"></span> about the <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span>-axis.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Write down an expression for the exact volume of revolution.</p></li><li><p>Use Simpson's rule with five ordinates to estimate this volume, giving your answer to 3 significant figures.</p></li></ol><p><strong>Prerequisite knowledge for Q14: Mean Value</strong></p>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle V=\\pi\\int_0^2[x\\ln(x+2)]^2\\,dx=\\pi\\int_0^2x^2(\\ln(x+2))^2\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=0.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The ordinates for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=0,0.5,1,1.5,2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0,0.25(\\ln2.5)^2,(\\ln3)^2,2.25(\\ln3.5)^2,4(\\ln4)^2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Thus</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}V&amp;\\approx\\frac{\\pi}{3}(0.5)\\{0+4(\\ln4)^2+4[0.25(\\ln2.5)^2+2.25(\\ln3.5)^2]+2(\\ln3)^2\\}\\\\&amp;=13.1\\quad(3\\text{ SF}).\\end{aligned}\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q14","number":14,"question":"<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Sketch the curve <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\" data-display=\"false\"></span> with equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=1+\\sin(\\tfrac{x}{2})\" data-display=\"false\"></span> for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0\\leq x\\leq 2\\pi\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>The region bounded by the curve <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, the coordinate axes, and the vertical straight line with equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=2\\pi\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is to be estimated by Simpson's rule using six equally spaced strips. Find, correct to six decimal places, an approximation to the area of this region.</p></li><li><p>Hence estimate the mean value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=1+\\sin(\\tfrac{x}{2})\" data-display=\"false\"></span> in the interval <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0\\leq x\\leq 2\\pi\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, giving your answer to five decimal places.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>The curve <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=1+\\sin(x/2)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> increases from <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(0,1)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(\\pi,2)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and decreases to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(2\\pi,1)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{6}=\\dfrac\\pi3\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Hence</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}A&amp;\\approx\\frac13\\cdot\\frac\\pi3\\{2+\\sin\\pi+4(3+\\sin\\tfrac\\pi6+\\sin\\tfrac\\pi2+\\sin\\tfrac{5\\pi}6)+2(2+\\sin\\tfrac\\pi3+\\sin\\tfrac{2\\pi}3)\\}\\\\&amp;=10.284912\\quad(6\\text{ DP}).\\end{aligned}\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p>The mean value is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\dfrac{A}{2\\pi}\\approx1.63689\" data-display=\"false\"></span> (5 DP).</p></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q15","number":15,"question":"<p>Given that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\ln a=\\int_1^a\\frac{1}{x}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, use Simpson's rule with seven ordinates to find an estimate for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\ln 7\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, giving your answer to four decimal places.</p><p><strong>Prerequisite knowledge for Q16--Q17: Maclaurin Series</strong></p>","steps":["<p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\dfrac{7-1}{6}=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The ordinates of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/x\" data-display=\"false\"></span> at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=1,2,3,4,5,6,7\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1,\\frac12,\\frac13,\\frac14,\\frac15,\\frac16,\\frac17\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Thus</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\ln7=\\int_1^7\\frac1x\\,dx\\approx\\frac13(1)\\left\\{1+\\frac17+4\\left(\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac16\\right)+2\\left(\\frac13+\\frac15\\right)\\right\\}=1.9587\\quad(4\\text{ DP}).\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q16","number":16,"question":"<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the first three non-zero terms in the Maclaurin expansion of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sin 2x\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Use Simpson's rule with four strips and this expansion to estimate <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\sin 2x\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, giving your answer to four decimal places.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sin2x=2x-\\dfrac{(2x)^3}{3!}+\\dfrac{(2x)^5}{5!}+\\cdots=2x-\\dfrac{4x^3}{3}+\\dfrac{4x^5}{15}+\\cdots\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, the polynomial values are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0,\\frac{1841}{3840},\\frac{101}{120},\\frac{1281}{1280},\\frac{14}{15}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Hence</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}\\int_0^1(2x-\\frac{4x^3}{3}+\\frac{4x^5}{15})\\,dx&amp;\\approx\\frac13(0.25)\\{0+\\frac{14}{15}+4(\\frac{1841}{3840}+\\frac{1281}{1280})+2(\\frac{101}{120})\\}\\\\&amp;=0.7115\\quad(4\\text{ DP}).\\end{aligned}\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q17","number":17,"question":"<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the first three non-zero terms in the expansion of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x^2\\ln(1+x)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Use Simpson's rule with four strips and this expansion to estimate <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^{0.4}x^2\\ln(1+x)\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, giving your answer to four decimal places.</p></li></ol><p><strong>Prerequisite knowledge for Q18: The Chain Rule</strong></p>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle x^2\\ln(1+x)\\approx x^2(x-\\frac{x^2}{2}+\\frac{x^3}{3})=x^3-\\frac{x^4}{2}+\\frac{x^5}{3}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>For <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=0,0.1,0.2,0.3,0.4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, the polynomial values are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0,\\frac{143}{150000},\\frac{137}{18750},\\frac{297}{12500},\\frac{512}{9375}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Thus</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}\\int_0^{0.4}(x^3-\\frac{x^4}{2}+\\frac{x^5}{3})\\,dx&amp;\\approx\\frac13(0.1)\\{0+\\frac{512}{9375}+4(\\frac{143}{150000}+\\frac{297}{12500})+2(\\frac{137}{18750})\\}\\\\&amp;=0.0056\\quad(4\\text{ DP}).\\end{aligned}\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q18","number":18,"question":"<p>The shaded regions on the diagram are bounded by the curve with equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=-x^x+4\" data-display=\"false\"></span> for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1\\leq x\\leq 2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, the tangent line at point <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\" data-display=\"false\"></span> where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=p\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, and the <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span>-axis. It is given that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p><figure class=\"diagram\"><img src=\"assets/2.64/questions/assets/diagrams/264-question-18.png\" alt=\"Original mathematical diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>State the coordinates of the point <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Use Simpson's rule with four strips to find an estimate for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_1^2(-x^x+4)\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, giving your answer to two decimal places.</p></li><li><p>Given that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x^x=e^{x\\ln x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\frac{d}{dx}(x^x)=x^x(1+\\ln x)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Find the exact form of the equation of the tangent line, giving your answer in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"ax+by=c\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"b\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"c\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are integers.</p></li><li><p>Hence find an approximate value for the total area of the shaded regions, giving your answer to two decimal places.</p></li></ol><p><strong>Prerequisite knowledge for Q19--Q21: Trapezium Rule, Mid-ordinate Rule and Reversing the Chain Rule or Integration by Substitution</strong></p>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=-1^1+4=3\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P=(1,3)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\dfrac{2-1}{4}=0.25\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The ordinates of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-x^x+4\" data-display=\"false\"></span> at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=1,1.25,1.5,1.75,2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"3,2.67829,2.16288,1.33733,0.\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>Hence</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_1^2(-x^x+4)\\,dx\\approx\\frac13(0.25)\\{3+0+4(2.67829+1.33733)+2(2.16288)\\}=1.95\\quad(2\\text{ DP}).\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x^x=e^{\\ln(x^x)}=e^{x\\ln x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\frac{d}{dx}(x^x)=\\frac{d}{dx}(e^{x\\ln x})=\\frac{d}{dx}(x\\ln x)e^{x\\ln x}=(x\\cdot\\frac1x+\\ln x)e^{x\\ln x}=x^x(1+\\ln x).\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p>At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=3\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. For <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=-x^x+4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-x^x(1+\\ln x)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, and at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span> the gradient is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y-3=-(x-1)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y+x=4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>The shaded area is the triangle of area <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac12(3)(3)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> less the area under the curve estimated in part (b): <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac12(3)(3)-1.95=2.55\" data-display=\"false\"></span> (2 DP).</p></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q19","number":19,"question":"<p>Let <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle I=\\int_0^2 x^2\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find an estimate for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"I\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, giving your answer as accurately as possible, using:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\" type=\"i\"><li><p>the trapezium rule with four strips, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T_4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>;</p></li><li><p>the mid-ordinate rule with four strips, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"M_4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>;</p></li><li><p>Simpson's rule with eight strips, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_8\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol></li><li><p>Find the exact value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"I\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\frac{2M_4+T_4}{3}=S_8\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\" type=\"i\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=0.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and the ordinates are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0,0.25,1,2.25,4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle T_4=\\frac12(0.5)\\{0+4+2(0.25+1+2.25)\\}=2.75\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Midpoints are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0.25,0.75,1.25,1.75\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, with function values <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0.0625,0.5625,1.5625,3.0625\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"M_4=0.5(0.0625+0.5625+1.5625+3.0625)=2.625.\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle S_8=\\frac13(0.5)\\{0+4+4(0.25+2.25)+2(1)\\}=\\frac83=2.\\dot6\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle I=\\int_0^2x^2\\,dx=[\\frac13x^3]_0^2=\\frac83\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\frac{2M_4+T_4}{3}=\\frac{2(2.625)+2.75}{3}=\\frac83=S_8\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q20","number":20,"question":"<p>Let <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle I=\\int_0^6\\sqrt{x+1}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find an estimate for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"I\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, giving your answer to six decimal places, using:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\" type=\"i\"><li><p>the trapezium rule with four strips, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T_4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>;</p></li><li><p>the mid-ordinate rule with four strips, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"M_4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>;</p></li><li><p>Simpson's rule with eight strips, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_8\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol></li><li><p>Find the exact value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"I\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\frac{2M_4+T_4}{3}=S_8\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\" type=\"i\"><li><p>With <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=1.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, the ordinates are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1,\\sqrt{2.5},2,\\sqrt{5.5},\\sqrt7\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle T_4=\\frac12(1.5)\\{1+\\sqrt7+2(\\sqrt{2.5}+2+\\sqrt{5.5})\\}=11.623834\" data-display=\"false\"></span> (6 DP).</p></li><li><p>The midpoints are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0.75,2.25,3.75,5.25\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, and values <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sqrt{1.75},\\sqrt{3.25},\\sqrt{4.75},\\sqrt{6.25}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle M_4=1.5(\\sqrt{1.75}+\\sqrt{3.25}+\\sqrt{4.75}+\\sqrt{6.25})=11.707651\" data-display=\"false\"></span> (6 DP).</p></li><li><p>With width <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0.75\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle S_8=\\frac13(0.75)\\{1+\\sqrt7+4(\\sqrt{1.75}+\\sqrt{3.25}+\\sqrt{4.75}+\\sqrt{6.25})+2(\\sqrt{2.5}+2+\\sqrt{5.5})\\}=11.679712\" data-display=\"false\"></span> (6 DP).</p></li></ol></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle I=\\int_0^6(x+1)^{1/2}\\,dx=[\\frac23(x+1)^{3/2}]_0^6=\\frac23(7\\sqrt7-1)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\frac{2M_4+T_4}{3}=\\frac{2(11.707651)+11.623834}{3}=11.679712=S_8\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.64-q21","number":21,"question":"<p>Let <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle I=\\int_a^b x^2\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"b\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are positive constants such that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a&lt;b\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Prove each of the following:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>The trapezium rule with two strips, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T_2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, is given by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\frac18(b-a)(3a^2+2ab+3b^2)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>The mid-ordinate rule with two strips, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"M_2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, is given by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\frac1{16}(b-a)(5a^2+6ab+5b^2)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Simpson's rule with four strips, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, is given by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\frac13(b-a)(a^2+ab+b^2)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>The exact value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"I\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is given by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\frac13(b-a)(a^2+ab+b^2)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\frac{2M_2+T_2}{3}=S_4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>With two strips, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\frac{b-a}{2}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and the ordinates are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a^2,\\frac{(a+b)^2}{4},b^2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Therefore</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}T_2&amp;=\\frac12\\cdot\\frac{b-a}{2}\\{a^2+b^2+2\\frac{(a+b)^2}{4}\\}\\\\&amp;=\\frac18(b-a)\\{2a^2+(a+b)^2+2b^2\\}=\\frac18(b-a)(3a^2+2ab+3b^2).\\end{aligned}\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p>The midpoint ordinates are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{(3a+b)^2}{16}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{(a+3b)^2}{16}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Hence</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}M_2&amp;=\\frac{b-a}{2}\\left\\{\\frac{(3a+b)^2}{16}+\\frac{(a+3b)^2}{16}\\right\\}\\\\&amp;=\\frac1{32}(b-a)(10a^2+12ab+10b^2)=\\frac1{16}(b-a)(5a^2+6ab+5b^2).\\end{aligned}\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p>Simpson's rule with four strips gives</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}S_4&amp;=\\frac13\\cdot\\frac{b-a}{4}\\left\\{a^2+b^2+4\\left(\\frac{(3a+b)^2+(a+3b)^2}{16}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{(a+b)^2}{4}\\right)\\right\\}\\\\&amp;=\\frac13\\cdot\\frac{b-a}{4}(4a^2+4ab+4b^2)=\\frac13(b-a)(a^2+ab+b^2).\\end{aligned}\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle I=\\int_a^b x^2\\,dx=[\\frac13x^3]_a^b=\\frac13(b^3-a^3)=\\frac13(b-a)(a^2+ab+b^2)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Using (a) and (b),</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2M_2+T_2}{3}&amp;=\\frac13\\left[\\frac18(b-a)(5a^2+6ab+5b^2)+\\frac18(b-a)(3a^2+2ab+3b^2)\\right]\\\\&amp;=\\frac13(b-a)(a^2+ab+b^2)=S_4.\\end{aligned}\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li></ol>"]}]